            ],
            "support": [
              {
                "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
                "quote": "As long as Ca ++ ions remain in the sarcoplasm to bind to troponin, which keeps the actin-binding sites “unshielded,” and as long as ATP is available to drive the cross-bridge cycling and the pulling of actin strands by myosin, the muscle fiber will continue to shorten to an anatomical limit.",
                "location": "paragraph_1"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "evidence_ids": [
          "E_d6fd3723dbb7"
        ],
        "support": [
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "This allows the myosin heads to bind to these exposed binding sites and form cross-bridges. The thin filaments are then pulled by the myosin heads to slide past the thick filaments toward the center of the sarcomere.",
            "location": "paragraph_11"
          },
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "ATP binding causes the myosin head to detach from the actin ( Figure 10.11 d ). After this occurs, ATP is converted to ADP and P i by the intrinsic ATPase activity of myosin.",
            "location": "paragraph_15"
          }
        ],
        "exceptions": [
          {
            "description": "If ATP runs out, myosin heads cannot detach from actin and the muscle cannot relax normally (rigor).",
            "evidence_quote": "In the absence of ATP, the myosin head will not detach from actin.",
            "location": "paragraph_14"
          }
        ],
